<section xml:id="sec-c-2-basic-identities">
  <title>Basic identities</title>

  <p>Trigonometric identities are equations that are true for every angle where both sides
are defined.</p>

  <p>They are useful because they let us change the form of an expression without changing
its value.  In calculus, this can turn a difficult derivative or integral into an easier one.</p>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-reciprocal-quotient-identities">
    <title>Reciprocal and quotient identities</title>

  <p>The reciprocal identities come directly from the definitions:</p>

  <md>
\sec x=\frac1{\cos x},
\qquad
\csc x=\frac1{\sin x},
\qquad
\cot x=\frac1{\tan x}.
</md>

  <p>The quotient identities are</p>

  <md>
\tan x=\frac{\sin x}{\cos x},
\qquad
\cot x=\frac{\cos x}{\sin x}.
</md>

  <p>Each identity is valid only where the denominators are nonzero.</p>

  <example xml:id="ex-sec-c-2-basic-identities-1">
    <title>Example</title>
    <statement>
  <p>Simplify</p>

  <md>\sin x\sec x.</md>

  <p>Since</p>

  <md>\sec x=\frac1{\cos x},</md>

  <p>we get</p>

  <md>\sin x\sec x
=
\sin x\cdot\frac1{\cos x}
=
\frac{\sin x}{\cos x}.</md>

  <p>Therefore</p>

  <md>
\sin x\sec x=\tan x
</md>

  <p>where <m>\cos x\neq0</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-pythagorean-identities">
    <title>Pythagorean identities</title>

  <p>The main identity comes from the unit circle:</p>

  <md>
\sin^2 x+\cos^2 x=1.
</md>

  <p>Here</p>

  <md>\sin^2 x</md>

  <p>means</p>

  <md>(\sin x)^2,</md>

  <p>not</p>

  <md>\sin(x^2).</md>

  <p>Dividing</p>

  <md>\sin^2 x+\cos^2 x=1</md>

  <p>by <m>\cos^2 x</m>, where <m>\cos x\neq0</m>, gives</p>

  <md>\tan^2 x+1=\sec^2 x.</md>

  <p>So</p>

  <md>
1+\tan^2 x=\sec^2 x.
</md>

  <p>Dividing by <m>\sin^2 x</m>, where <m>\sin x\neq0</m>, gives</p>

  <md>1+\cot^2 x=\csc^2 x.</md>

  <p>Thus</p>

  <md>
1+\cot^2 x=\csc^2 x.
</md>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-c-2-basic-identities-0">
    <tabular halign="left">
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><strong>Pythagorean identities</strong></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\sin^2 x+\cos^2 x=1</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>1+\tan^2 x=\sec^2 x</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>1+\cot^2 x=\csc^2 x</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <example xml:id="ex-sec-c-2-basic-identities-2">
    <title>Example</title>
    <statement>
  <p>Simplify</p>

  <md>1-\cos^2 x.</md>

  <p>From</p>

  <md>\sin^2 x+\cos^2 x=1,</md>

  <p>we get</p>

  <md>1-\cos^2 x=\sin^2 x.</md>

  <p>So</p>

  <md>
1-\cos^2 x=\sin^2 x.
</md>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="ex-sec-c-2-basic-identities-3">
    <title>Example</title>
    <statement>
  <p>Simplify</p>

  <md>\sec^2 x-\tan^2 x.</md>

  <p>Using</p>

  <md>1+\tan^2 x=\sec^2 x,</md>

  <p>we get</p>

  <md>\sec^2 x-\tan^2 x=1.</md>

  <p>Thus</p>

  <md>
\sec^2 x-\tan^2 x=1.
</md>
    </statement>
  </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-even-odd-trig-identities">
    <title>Even and odd identities</title>

  <p>On the unit circle, replacing <m>x</m> by <m>-x</m> reflects the point across the <m>x</m>-axis.
The <m>x</m>-coordinate stays the same, while the <m>y</m>-coordinate changes sign.</p>

  <p>Therefore</p>

  <md>
\cos(-x)=\cos x
</md>

  <p>and</p>

  <md>
\sin(-x)=-\sin x.
</md>

  <p>Since</p>

  <md>\tan x=\frac{\sin x}{\cos x},</md>

  <p>we also have</p>

  <md>
\tan(-x)=-\tan x.
</md>

  <p>So cosine is even, while sine and tangent are odd.</p>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-c-2-basic-identities-1">
    <tabular halign="left">
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><strong>identity</strong></cell>
        <cell><strong>meaning</strong></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\cos(-x)=\cos x</m></cell>
        <cell>cosine is even</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\sin(-x)=-\sin x</m></cell>
        <cell>sine is odd</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\tan(-x)=-\tan x</m></cell>
        <cell>tangent is odd</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <example xml:id="ex-sec-c-2-basic-identities-4">
    <title>Example</title>
    <statement>
  <p>Simplify</p>

  <md>\sin(-x)\cos(-x).</md>

  <p>Use the even and odd identities:</p>

  <md>\sin(-x)\cos(-x)
=
(-\sin x)(\cos x).</md>

  <p>Therefore</p>

  <md>
\sin(-x)\cos(-x)=-\sin x\cos x.
</md>
    </statement>
  </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-using-identities-carefully">
    <title>Using identities carefully</title>

  <p>Trigonometric identities can simplify expressions, but they must be used with the same
care as algebraic identities.</p>

  <warning xml:id="warn-sec-c-2-basic-identities-43">
    <title>Warning</title>
      <p>Never divide by a trigonometric expression unless you know it is not zero.</p>
      <p>For example, the equation</p>
        <md>\sin x\cos x=\sin x</md>
      <p>cannot be simplified by blindly dividing by <m>\sin x</m>.  Some solutions may have
<m>\sin x=0</m>.</p>
  </warning>

  <example xml:id="ex-sec-c-2-basic-identities-5">
    <title>Example</title>
    <statement>
  <p>Solve</p>

  <md>\sin x\cos x=\sin x</md>

  <p>on</p>

  <md>0\le x&lt;2\pi.</md>

  <p>Move all terms to one side:</p>

  <md>\sin x\cos x-\sin x=0.</md>

  <p>Factor:</p>

  <md>\sin x(\cos x-1)=0.</md>

  <p>Thus</p>

  <md>\sin x=0
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
\cos x-1=0.</md>

  <p>On <m>0\le x&lt;2\pi</m>,</p>

  <md>\sin x=0</md>

  <p>at</p>

  <md>x=0,\ \pi.</md>

  <p>Also,</p>

  <md>\cos x=1</md>

  <p>at</p>

  <md>x=0.</md>

  <p>So the solutions are</p>

  <md>
x=0,\ \pi.
</md>

  <p>If we had divided by <m>\sin x</m>, we would have lost the solution <m>x=\pi</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  </subsection>

</section>
